-    "GLOSSARY.md": "# Ticket Execution\n\nThe workflow for carrying approved software work through implementation, verification, and local integration.\n\n## Language\n\n**Spec**:\nThe agreed description of intended behavior, constraints, and acceptance requirements for a body of work.\n_Avoid_: Plan (when referring to required outcomes)\n\n**Ticket**:\nA bounded piece of work under an approved spec, with its own acceptance requirements and declared dependencies on other tickets.\n\n**Approved plan**:\nThe human-accepted version of a spec, its tickets, milestone membership, and acceptance conditions.\n\n**Milestone**:\nA planned group of ticket outcomes with human acceptance conditions that gates further feature work and promotion.\n\n**QA decision**:\nThe human's recorded acceptance or request for corrections for a specific milestone candidate.\n\n**Skill selection**:\nThe skills approved for a role's work, combining its required role skills, project defaults, and any applicable ticket additions.\n\n**Run**:\nAn execution of the harness against an approved spec and selected tickets, together with its recorded outcomes.\n\n**Implementation attempt**:\nA worker assignment to produce a ticket implementation and submit its result. Development and testing iterations within that assignment belong to the same attempt.\n\n**Repair attempt**:\nAn implementation attempt made in response to a rejected submission, using the current changes and the reported failures as its starting point.\n\n**Blocking finding**:\nA reviewer-reported issue that must be resolved before a ticket may be integrated. It can trigger repair without immediately putting the ticket into the blocked state.\n\n**Optional suggestion**:\nA reviewer-reported improvement that does not prevent integration or require an automatic repair attempt.\n\n**Harness**:\nThe system responsible for coordinating ticket execution, verification, bounded repairs, and integration for approved work.\n_Avoid_: Coordinator agent, dispatcher agent\n\n**Target repository**:\nThe Git repository in which the harness carries out approved work. Its project commands, standards, and constraints govern that work.\n_Avoid_: Client repository\n\n**Integration branch**:\nThe local destination branch into which verified changes are combined. Its role may be a feature branch or a development branch; integration does not imply permission to publish.\n\n**Feature branch**:\nThe integration branch that accumulates verified ticket changes for one approved feature before milestone acceptance.\n\n**Development branch**:\nThe shared integration branch that receives accepted milestone candidates from feature branches, conventionally named `dev`.\n\n**Promotion**:\nThe advancement of the development branch to a verified milestone candidate accepted by the human.\n\n**Candidate merge**:\nThe proposed combination of source changes and a destination branch, evaluated before that destination advances. A ticket candidate targets the feature branch; a milestone candidate targets the development branch.\n\n**Review work item**:\nAn identified assessment of a defined source revision and scope, linked to its reports and outstanding findings. Completing the assessment does not itself resolve its findings or accept a milestone.\n\n**Review report**:\nThe preserved result of one reviewer assignment against an identified source and scope, including inspected coverage, findings and unresolved assumptions.\n\n**Finding disposition**:\nThe recorded treatment of a review finding, with its reason, authority and supporting evidence. Its history connects the original concern to a verified fix, linked deferral or explicit human decision.\n\n**Review decision**:\nA human's recorded choice about consequential review risk, scope, ownership or deferral. It is separate from a QA decision accepting an exact milestone candidate.\n\n**Architecture checkpoint**:\nA required cumulative assessment of responsibility and invariant ownership across an affected workflow before specified integration or milestone-readiness progression.\n",
-    "docs/ticket-harness.md": "# Scripted sequential ticket harness\n\nTickets 01\u201309 execute explicitly selected approved tickets sequentially with scripted external agents,\nvalidate its requirements and reports, bind verification to exact revisions, and\nrepair rejected submissions within a configured allowance, stopping immediately\nwhen a valid report requires a human decision outside approved contracts.\nOptional review suggestions remain visible without requiring repair or delaying integration.\nStage timeouts stop stalled processes and their process-group descendants.\nContinuing unrelated work after a blocked ticket, live Codex, and an operating-system\nsandbox remain later numbered tickets. Run only trusted scripted processes and\nproject commands.\n\n## Finding the code\n\nThe runtime groups code by the work it owns:\n\n- `runner.py` coordinates the run. Its `_Run.check` function runs commands,\n  validates agent reports, and keeps check results tied to the code they assessed.\n  It also protects saved evidence and reconnects restored results to the run record.\n- `tickets.py` chooses eligible tickets and carries out each ticket's complete\n  implementation, verification, and bounded repair cycle.\n- `qa.py` prepares milestone candidates, requests human review, checks decisions,\n  and handles deliberate stop/continuation. Stop, continuation, and promotion use\n  the same check for unchanged review requests and evidence.\n- `git.py` owns worktrees, submission commits, merge validation, and branch updates.\n- `records.py` owns process cleanup, output artifacts, file protection, and atomic\n  record writes. `agents.py` owns assignment content and report formats.\n- `inputs.py` and `plans.py` capture approved inputs and reject invalid requirements,\n  configuration, dependency graphs, and milestone plans before execution.\n\nThe public commands and saved record formats remain the caller and test contracts.\nThese internal divisions do not change ticket repair limits, milestone approval\nrequirements, or which failures stop the run.\n\n## Python and checks\n\nUse the [testing policy](agents/testing.md) to select focused development checks,\npreserve the full-suite completion gate, and capture evidence without redundant runs.\n\nUse the reusable micromamba Python environment described in\n[Python environment guidance](agents/python-environment.md). The runtime uses only\nPython 3.12+, Git, and POSIX process groups (the fixtures also use Linux `/proc`\nto observe child termination). From this checkout, `python -m ticket_harness` requires no\ninstallation. For an installed `ticket-harness` command, install the project with\n`python -m pip install '.[dev]'`. The `dev` extra pins pytest, mypy, and Ruff;\npackaging uses a pinned setuptools build backend.\n\nFor isolated check tools, create a disposable micromamba prefix:\n\n```bash\nmicromamba --root-prefix /tmp/ticket-harness-mamba create --yes \\\n  --prefix /tmp/ticket-harness-env --channel conda-forge \\\n  python=3.12.16 pytest=9.2.0 mypy=1.19.1 ruff=0.14.10\n```\n\nWith that environment's `bin` directory on `PATH`, run:\n\n```bash\npython -m pytest\npython -m mypy\npython -m ruff check ticket_harness tests examples\npython -m ruff format --check ticket_harness tests examples\n```\n\nTests use the public CLI, scripted external processes, real temporary Git\nrepositories, and observable Git/record artifacts. No network or model is needed.\n\n## Run the minimal fixture\n\nFrom this checkout, with the micromamba environment's Python on `PATH`:\n\n```bash\nfixture=$(mktemp -d /tmp/ticket-harness-fixture.XXXXXX)\ncp -R examples/scripted \"$fixture/target\"\ngit -C \"$fixture/target\" init -b main\ngit -C \"$fixture/target\" add .\ngit -C \"$fixture/target\" -c user.name=Fixture \\\n  -c user.email=fixture@example.invalid commit -m 'Fixture baseline'\ngit -C \"$fixture/target\" branch integration\npython -m ticket_harness run --config \"$fixture/target/project.json\" \\\n  --output \"$fixture/passing-run\"\npython -m ticket_harness status \"$fixture/passing-run\"\n```\n\n`run` intentionally approves the chosen requirements and commands. Exit 0 means\nDone in legacy selection mode, or final accepted QA promotion in milestone mode; nonzero means invalid inputs, environment failure, or incomplete work, as\nrecorded in `outcome` and `failure`. `status RUN_DIRECTORY` prints `run.json`\nwithout running processes or modifying source or records; exit 0 means the\nrecord was read, including for an incomplete run.\n\nTo demonstrate rejection, repeat with a fresh fixture and add `\"candidate\"` as\nthe last argument in its configured `suite`. Baseline passes, but the candidate\nsuite exits nonzero. The run is incomplete, the integration branch stays at the\nbaseline, and the submitted/candidate revisions and logs remain inspectable.\nAlternatively, append `[\"--reject\", \"spec\"]` or `[\"--reject\", \"standards\"]` to\n`agent` for a blocking review.\n\n## Approved milestone plans\n\nSupply `\"plan\": \"plan.json\"` in project configuration to execute fixed\nmilestones into `integration`, which serves as the feature branch. Omission retains\nthe earlier explicit ticket-selection contract. A plan is a separate approved JSON\ninput, snapshotted and SHA-256 recorded alongside the spec, tickets and configuration.\nStarting `run` approves these exact inputs; the `approval` field records the human\nbundle-approval reference and never asserts milestone QA acceptance.\n\nThis minimal example uses two required tickets and two groups:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"v1\",\n  \"approval\": \"human approved bundle v1 in planning session\",\n  \"development\": \"dev\",\n  \"required_tickets\": [\"01\", \"02\"],\n  \"milestones\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"M1\",\n      \"tickets\": [\"01\"],\n      \"qa\": {\n        \"setup\": \"Inspect the disposable target repository\",\n        \"actions\": [\"Exercise the first capability\"],\n        \"pass_conditions\": [\"The first capability produces its approved result\"]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"M2\",\n      \"tickets\": [\"02\"],\n      \"qa\": {\n        \"setup\": \"Inspect the complete feature\",\n        \"actions\": [\"Exercise both capabilities together\"],\n        \"pass_conditions\": [\"All required outcomes satisfy overall feature acceptance\"]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"final_milestone\": \"M2\",\n  \"final_coverage\": [\"01\", \"02\"],\n  \"roles\": {\n    \"worker\": {\"model\": \"approved-worker-model\"},\n    \"spec\": {\"model\": \"approved-spec-model\"},\n    \"standards\": {\"model\": \"approved-standards-model\"}\n  },\n  \"worker_overrides\": {\n    \"02\": {\"reasoning\": \"High\", \"reason\": \"Approved complexity override\"}\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll shown fields except `worker_overrides` are required. Unknown object fields\nand duplicate JSON keys reject. Strings must be nonempty without NUL. Ticket IDs\nare numeric strings normalized like Markdown heading IDs. Each required ticket\nmust exist in `tickets` and belong to exactly one nonempty milestone. Context-only\nbundle tickets are allowed; external prerequisites still need verified\n`completed_runs` evidence. Dependencies within a milestone or pointing to an\nearlier milestone are valid; dependencies into a later milestone reject, including\ntransitive violations through context tickets. The final milestone must be last,\nand `final_coverage` must explicitly contain every required ticket exactly once,\nincluding earlier groups cumulatively. Each QA object requires nonempty `setup`,\n`actions` and `pass_conditions` (the last two are arrays of nonempty strings).\n\nEach role requires an explicit model string. Reasoning defaults independently to\n`Medium` for worker and `High` for Spec and Standards; explicit `Low`, `Medium` or\n`High` values are supported. Optional `context_limit` is a positive integer active\ncontext target. Each ticket worker override must name a required ticket, supply a\nnonempty `reason` and at least one setting (`model`, `reasoning`, `context_limit`).\nIts effective settings apply equally to implementation and repair; reviewers keep\ntheir independent profiles. `run.json` captures normalized `plan` and effective\n`worker_settings` for every required ticket. These are approved profile records;\nthe scripted command contract is unchanged, and this slice does not enforce model\nselection or context occupancy. Live profile enforcement remains future work.\n\nOptional Markdown header fields `Plan version`, `Milestone`, and `Worker reasoning`\nuse plain scalar values exactly matching the plan version, group ID and effective\nreasoning (for example `**Milestone:** M1`). Duplicate declarations reject even\nwhen identical. Links, explanatory suffixes or inheritance prose in these three\nfields are unsupported in this runtime contract and reject against the explicit\nplan values. The shared planning reference defines authoring policy; its documents\nare not automatically executable plan JSON.\n\n`ticket` must identify a member of the first milestone. Omit `selected_tickets`\nto select that whole group, or explicitly list exactly its members. Later groups\ncannot be selected. Among ready tickets the harness follows the fixed order in\nthat group's `tickets` array, honoring actual dependencies; each implementation\nstarts from the latest verified feature revision. Existing baseline, lightweight\nchecks, separate reviews, bounded repairs and exact ticket-candidate gates remain\nmandatory. Validation completes before worktrees, checks, agents or ref changes.\n`development` must name an existing local branch distinct from `integration`;\nits starting revision is recorded. Every required ticket must be ready-for-agent,\nand dependencies outside the plan require verified completed-run evidence, including\nfor later groups. These checks happen before execution.\n\nAfter the current group's tickets become Done, the harness prepares a milestone\ncandidate combining the exact feature tip and the **current** development tip.\nThe starting development revision remains recorded separately. During preparation,\n`outcome: \"preparing_milestone\"` and milestone state `preparing` remain observable.\nIt runs `suite`\nagain against that exact candidate with `TICKET_HARNESS_STAGE=milestone-regression`,\nusing the configured `candidate` timeout. This is separate from each ticket's\nrequired `candidate` suite; none of the ticket gates are skipped or reused as\nmilestone regression evidence.\n\nSuccessful preparation keeps the foreground process running indefinitely with\n`outcome: \"awaiting_human_qa\"`,\n`feature_complete: false`, and a `milestone` object containing `id`,\n`state: \"awaiting_human_qa\"`, `feature_revision`, `development_revision` and\n`candidate_revision`. The `tickets` mapping includes all required tickets; later\ngroups remain `Pending` with attempt zero. `selected_tickets` contains only the\ncurrent group in numeric order. Even a single final group with all tickets Done\nwaits for human acceptance.\n\n`qa_request` points to `qa-request.json` in the run directory, outside source\nworktrees. It records unique request/run identities, the run directory, approved\nplan version/approval and captured plan identity, milestone, feature/development\nbranches and revisions, exact candidate/worktree, planned human setup/actions/pass\nconditions, and the actual passing regression gate with command, revision,\nexit/timeout result and output artifact paths. This request supplies no human\ndecision. Candidate preparation never advances development or starts later work.\n\nBefore issuing the request the harness revalidates approved inputs, all verified\nsources and both branch tips. Regression failure, candidate/source mutation, or\nexternal branch movement stops preparation with `outcome: \"incomplete\"` and\nmilestone state `preparation_failed`, retaining candidate/source/gate evidence and\nexternal refs. Completed tickets remain Done. No QA request is issued. Preparation\nhas no automatic repair budget and does not consume ticket attempts.\n\nClean automatic merges, including nonoverlapping edits in the same file, are\nsupported. An independent plain merge in an isolated bare Git directory ignores\nproject hooks, configuration and attributes to detect source-changing conflict\nresolutions. Git must support `merge-tree --write-tree` and the global\n`--attr-source` option; unavailable support fails preparation closed. An unresolved\nconflict or a candidate differing from the clean merge stops with preserved source.\nCustom merge-driver resolutions and merge-hook edits require renewed applicable\nchecks and reviews before QA; existing ticket approvals cannot authorize them.\nThis slice stops for that renewal rather than launching conflict-repair workers.\nAutomatic conflict repair/renewal orchestration is not provided.\n\nThe foreground runner watches separate run-directory `qa-decision.json`. The exact\nJSON contract has two fields: `decision`, the case-sensitive string `\"APPROVED\"`,\nand `request`, the complete JSON object from the current `qa-request.json`, including\nevery identity, human check and regression-evidence field. Object key order and\nwhitespace do not matter; field names, value types and values must match. Duplicate\nkeys, extra fields, malformed JSON, incidental approval text, missing fields, stale\nrequests and replayed approvals cannot promote or unlock work. Write decisions\natomically (write a temporary sibling file, then rename it) after the human accepts\nthat exact request. The human/QA session owns this artifact; agent assignments and\nimplementation/repair output cannot authorize QA. Requests, decisions, run records\nand prior evidence are protected across external process boundaries. This is the\nexisting trusted-process boundary, not an operating-system sandbox.\n\n`qa_decision` names the watched path. `qa_decision_status` reports `missing`,\n`unreadable`, `rejected` or `consumed`; `qa_observations` retains each distinct read\ndecision's actual bytes in an artifact, SHA-256, request identity and validation\ndiagnostic. A regular file is required; directories and symlinks remain unreadable\nwaits. Invalid or absent decisions leave `awaiting_human_qa` and do not consume\nrepairs or use agent/check timeouts. `status RUN_DIRECTORY` observes these waits.\n\nBefore consuming exact acceptance, the runner revalidates captured inputs, all\napplicable verified sources, the unchanged request and retained evidence bytes.\nA single Git ref transaction compares both expected feature/development tips and\nadvances both to the exact tested candidate. Neither branch may be checked out in\nany caller worktree. External ref movement is preserved; stale source/input/request/\nevidence or branch state stops with `incomplete` and milestone `promotion_failed`,\nrequiring renewed candidate checks and acceptance. Ticket Done states remain intact.\nNo ref partially advances if either expected tip mismatches.\n\n`qa_decisions` records consumed decision artifact/hash/request identities; `milestones`\nrecords promoted groups. Requests are archived per group under `artifacts`; the\ncurrent `qa-request.json` is replaced for the next group. Consumption admits only\nthat next fixed group, starting workers from the aligned feature revision and\nretaining every per-ticket gate and repair allowance. Later regression and candidate\nartifacts use distinct milestone names. All required tickets Done still leaves\n`feature_complete: false` until final acceptance promotes. Only then does the run\nexit 0 with `outcome: \"completed\"` and `feature_complete: true`.\n\nAt an active planned QA wait, request a deliberate clean stop from another shell:\n\n```bash\npython -m ticket_harness stop RUN_DIRECTORY\npython -m ticket_harness status RUN_DIRECTORY\npython -m ticket_harness continue RUN_DIRECTORY\n```\n\nThe installed command supports the same operations: `ticket-harness stop RUN_DIRECTORY`\nand `ticket-harness continue RUN_DIRECTORY`. `stop` exits 0 after acknowledging\n`outcome: \"stop_requested\"`; wait for the owning foreground process to exit **2**\nand status to show `outcome: \"stopped_at_qa\"` before continuing. Exit 2 means a\nsuccessful planned stop, with `feature_complete: false`, rather than completed work.\nStop is supported only during an active QA wait; it never terminates a worker,\nreview, check or merge. An approval racing a stop can win first; the acknowledgement\nalone does not establish a stopped checkpoint. Inspect the actual run outcome.\n\nThe durable `qa-checkpoint.json` and immutable `checkpoints/<checkpoint_id>.json`\narchive bind the normalized approved configuration, typed run state, plan/version,\nDone/attempt/consumed-decision identities, every applicable verified source,\nrequest bytes and original evidence hashes. New decision observations are also\nbound. Prior checkpoints remain archived across repeated stops. `continue` acquires\nexclusive ownership of the run directory, validates captured approved sources and\nsnapshots, checkpoint/record/request/evidence identities, clean verified trees,\nand both expected branch tips and checked-out status before changing the run.\nChanged or inconsistent state rejects with exit 1 and a unique diagnostic under\n`rejections/`, preserving prior records, checkpoints, artifacts and refs.\nDo not move the run directory or edit its checkpoint/history to resume it.\n\nContinuation watches the same exact separate QA decision contract, including a\ndecision written while stopped. It preserves Done tickets and previous artifacts,\nskips their assignments and baseline, and admits only the approved next group.\nIt remains foreground and waits indefinitely at subsequent QA checkpoints; only\nfinal acceptance exits 0/completed/true. Each run has one active owner, serialized\nby a POSIX lock on the directory inode. Concurrent continuation rejects rather\nthan repeating assignments or consuming/promoting acceptance twice. The operating\nsystem releases ownership on process exit, but this does not make an arbitrary\ninterruption recoverable: only deliberately persisted `stopped_at_qa` is eligible.\nNo arbitrary mid-stage crash recovery, background launch, or process sandbox is\nprovided. A new `run` cannot use recorded ticket completion to skip first-group QA.\n`status` remains observational and does not revalidate stopped candidates.\nDevelopment checkpoints for building this harness are separate from these\ntarget-repository runtime plans.\n\n## Canonical input contract\n\n`examples/scripted/project.json` remains a valid minimal example. Configuration is\na JSON object with the following fields. Unknown fields and duplicate keys\n(including inside nested objects) are rejected instead of silently ignored.\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `repository` | Git target path, relative to the configuration file or absolute. |\n| `base` | Starting commit/ref; must equal the existing integration branch tip. |\n| `integration` | Existing local destination branch, not checked out in any worktree. |\n| `spec`, `ticket` | Approved Markdown paths, relative to the target or absolute. |\n| `tickets` | Optional nonempty array of all Markdown ticket paths in this requirements bundle, including `ticket`. Defaults to `[ticket]`. It provides dependency context, not a multi-ticket execution selection. Paths must be distinct after resolution. |\n| `selected_tickets` | Optional nonempty array of ticket paths to execute, relative to the target or absolute. Defaults to `[ticket]`; must include `ticket` and be a subset of `tickets`, with distinct resolved paths. Bundle context alone never selects work. |\n| `plan` | Optional approved milestone-plan JSON path, relative to the target repository or absolute. Enables the milestone contract below. |\n| `completed_runs` | Optional array of earlier harness `run.json` paths, relative to the configuration file or absolute; defaults to `[]`. These provide prerequisite completion evidence, described below. |\n| `standards` | Paths for applicable project rules, AGENTS instructions, ADRs, and policy; list them explicitly. |\n| `agent` | Argument array for the trusted scripted executable, used freshly for each role. |\n| `suite` | Argument array for broader baseline and candidate checks. |\n| `checks` | Nonempty mapping of named lightweight checks to argument arrays. |\n| `max_repairs` | Optional nonnegative integer; defaults to `2` repairs after the initial assignment. `0` stops on the first rejection. Booleans, floats, strings, and null are invalid. |\n| `timeouts` | Optional object overriding stage allowances in seconds; omitted keys use the defaults below. Every supplied value must be a positive finite JSON number no greater than `604800` (one week). Booleans, strings, null, unknown stages, and unsafe numeric ranges are rejected before any worker/check starts. |\n\nAll fields except `tickets`, `selected_tickets`, `completed_runs`, `max_repairs`, `timeouts`, and `plan` are required. Paths, refs, and\nbranch names must be nonempty strings; `standards` is an array of nonempty paths\n(it may be empty). Approved files must exist and be readable. `checks` is a\nnonempty mapping. Check names match `[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:-[A-Za-z0-9]+)*`.\nEvery command, including unused named checks, must be a nonempty argument array\nof nonempty strings without NUL characters, with an available executable.\nExecutable availability is checked without invoking it: names resolve on `PATH`;\nabsolute paths must name executable files. Relative executable paths must name\nregular files tracked with executable mode (`100755`) in the approved base tree;\nuntracked files, caller-only permission changes, symlinks, and paths escaping the\nworktree cannot supply a relative executable. Relative executable paths must not\ncontain `..` segments or end in `/` or `/.`. A bare name resolving through a relative\n`PATH` entry is rejected; use an absolute executable path or a tracked `./path`.\nCommands execute directly, without a shell, in their assigned detached worktree.\nRelative executable/script arguments must also be available at the approved base\nrevision; preflight does not interpret arbitrary executable arguments or predict\nruntime check success. `TICKET_HARNESS_STAGE` identifies the stage for commands.\n\nMarkdown uses the existing local tracker conventions:\n\n```markdown\n# 02: Follow-up\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n**Blocked by:** [01: Change value](01-value.md)\n**Checks:** ticket\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The follow-up has an observable result.\n```\n\n- The header has exactly one `# NN: Title`, with a nonempty title and positive\n  decimal numeric ID. Leading zeroes normalize to at least two digits (`1`, `01`,\n  and `001` all mean `01`). IDs must be unique across the bundle. A numeric\n  `NN-` filename prefix must agree with the heading. Optional `ID:` and `Title:`\n  metadata must agree too.\n- Header metadata precedes the first `##` section. Both `Status: value` and\n  `**Status:** value` forms work (likewise other fields). Whitespace around\n  values is trimmed. Duplicate fields are rejected, even when repeated values\n  agree. Other descriptive header fields such as `Scope:` remain prose.\n  YAML front matter is unsupported and rejected; it cannot override the header.\n- `Status:` must use a configured local readiness label: `needs-triage`,\n  `needs-info`, `ready-for-agent`, `ready-for-human`, or `wontfix`. Every\n  selected ticket must be `ready-for-agent`. These are triage labels, never\n  execution states or proof of prerequisite completion.\n- `Blocked by:` is required. No dependencies may be written as `None`, `None.`,\n  or the established `None (can start immediately).` form (the final period is\n  optional). Otherwise use comma-separated IDs, `NN: Title` references, or\n  `[NN: Title](relative-ticket-path.md)` links; a numeric-only link label also\n  works. Titles containing commas can be referenced by ID alone. Supplied\n  titles and resolved links must agree with the referenced ticket. Every\n  dependency must exist in `tickets`; cycles, including self-cycles and cycles\n  in unselected work, invalidate the bundle.\n- Exactly one `## Acceptance criteria` section must contain at least one\n  nonempty `- [ ]`, `- [x]`, or `* [ ]` checklist item (`X` also works). Empty\n  checklist items are rejected even beside valid ones. Checked boxes carry\n  acceptance text, never harness Done state. Fenced code examples and appended\n  `## Comments` history do not supply requirements or metadata.\n- Optional `Checks:` lists comma-separated configured check names with no\n  duplicates. Omission means all configured checks. Empty or unknown references\n  are rejected. Only referenced checks execute and appear in each assignment's\n  `required_checks`; the broader `suite` always runs at baseline and candidate.\n\n`run.json.requirements` records the normalized bundle, keyed by canonical ID.\nEach entry has `id`, `title`, `readiness`, `dependencies` (canonical IDs),\n`acceptance` (checklist text), `checks` (names), and `source` (absolute path).\nOriginal Markdown/configuration bytes remain the approved snapshots.\n\nReadiness and prerequisite satisfaction are independent. Include every prerequisite\nin `tickets`. Select prerequisites for execution in this run or provide their\nearlier `run.json` records in `completed_runs`. Each completion record must report\n`outcome: \"completed\"`; every entry in its `tickets` mapping must be `Done`,\nwith a `done_revision` already ancestral to the configured base. Legacy records\nwithout `tickets` use their single `ticket` entry. Every completed ticket must\nexist in the current bundle, and its approved source path and SHA-256 must match\nthe current prerequisite file. Missing, incomplete, stale, duplicate, or\nunintegrated completion evidence rejects the run. A changed prerequisite file\nrequires fresh completion evidence; checked acceptance boxes alone never suffice.\nCompletion records are trusted local harness artifacts, not an authenticated\nprovenance system. Explicitly selected prerequisites must reach Done again in\nthis run; external evidence cannot unlock their dependents early.\n\nFor example, add these fields to select the follow-up above after the earlier\nfixture run completed (and use `base: \"integration\"`):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ticket\": \"02-follow-up.md\",\n  \"tickets\": [\"01-value.md\", \"02-follow-up.md\"],\n  \"completed_runs\": [\"../passing-run/run.json\"]\n}\n```\n\nTo execute a dependency graph in one run, explicitly select its tickets:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ticket\": \"02-follow-up.md\",\n  \"tickets\": [\"01-value.md\", \"02-follow-up.md\", \"03-independent.md\", \"04-human-context.md\"],\n  \"selected_tickets\": [\"02-follow-up.md\", \"03-independent.md\", \"01-value.md\"]\n}\n```\n\nAfter preflight validates the entire bundle and all commands, the broader starting\nbaseline runs once. At each selection, the harness chooses the lowest **numeric**\ncanonical ID among Ready selected tickets, independently of configuration order.\nUnselected agent-ready and human-ready context never executes automatically.\nA ticket is Pending until all its prerequisites are either externally verified\nat the starting base or Done in this run. Integration promotion precedes Done and\nunlocking. There is at most one active implementation ticket, including repairs;\nits checks, both reviews and exact-candidate broader suite complete before the\nnext selection. Every new ticket starts at the latest verified integration tip,\nincluding already completed independent changes. Its assignment `base_revision`\nis fixed to that tip across its own repairs. Each assignment's `inputs.ticket`\nis the active ticket's approved content; other bundle snapshots remain context.\n`required_checks` and `check_results` contain that ticket's checks and reports,\nplus the shared baseline, without transferring earlier tickets' findings or approvals.\nThe durable record retains all earlier evidence.\n\nSuccess requires every selection to reach Done. This slice stops the run on the\nfirst blocked ticket or shared environment failure; selected dependents remain\nPending and unrelated selections retain their observed Ready state. Continuing\nunrelated work after a block belongs to ticket 29. A shared baseline failure\nlaunches no workers and consumes no repairs; the compatibility `ticket` entry\n(the configured primary before execution) becomes Blocked and other selections\nretain their pre-baseline states. Integration changes made outside the harness\nare never overwritten by candidate promotion.\n\nA preflight rejection returns nonzero with `outcome: \"invalid-input\"`, an `inputs` failure\nmessage naming the offending field/ticket, no gates or assignments, and attempt\n0. No worker/check processes start, no Git worktrees are added, and integration\nreferences remain unchanged. The new output directory may contain diagnostic\nrecords and snapshots. Git/environment preflight also retains the original\nbase/integration/ref checks before execution.\n\nThe output must be a new directory outside the target repository and separate\nfrom approved input files. Each approved file, including configuration, bundle\ntickets, completion records, and standards, is copied verbatim to `inputs/`;\n`run.json` stores its SHA-256 and\noriginal path. Agent-editable source lives separately under `worktrees/`.\n\n## Assignment and report contracts\n\nEach assignment starts a fresh process with no conversation history. Standard\ninput is one JSON object containing `role` (`implementation`, `spec`, or\n`standards`), `ticket`, `attempt` (1 for the initial assignment, then 2, 3, ...), `base_revision`, `revision`, role\n`instructions`, approved `inputs` as text, `required_checks`, actual\n`check_results` with artifact paths, and a `report_contract`. The full assignment\nis saved before invocation. Reviewers can inspect `git diff BASE REVISION`.\nRepairs retain role `implementation`. Their `revision` is the starting source\ncommit, while `base_revision` remains the original approved integration revision.\n`repair_context` is null for the initial assignment and reviews; for repairs it\ncontains the previous `failure` (stage/message) and all recorded\n`rejected_revisions` (submission and candidate identities). `check_results`\ncontains the complete prior gate history, including parsed findings, diagnostics,\nand readable stdout/stderr paths. Failed Git operations include their diagnostic\nin the failure message. Workers receive current working contents in a fresh\ndetached worktree, including uncommitted edits after invalid reports; previous\nworktrees remain inspectable. Reports must copy the new attempt identity exactly.\n\nStandard output must contain exactly one JSON object, with diagnostics on\nstandard error. Missing output, malformed JSON, duplicate keys (at any depth),\nnonfinite numbers, extra JSON documents, and incorrect field types are rejected.\nExtra diagnostic metadata such as `pid` is allowed but has no authority.\nObject/array nesting is limited to 100 levels (the report object is level 1)\nto leave serialization headroom for durable records and subsequent assignments.\nThe assignment's `report_contract` lists required fields, field types, permitted\noutcomes, and finding requirements. Existing complete scripted reports remain valid.\n\nEvery report copies these identity fields **exactly**, including JSON types:\n\n| Field | Required value/type |\n| --- | --- |\n| `role` | Assignment string: `implementation`, `spec`, or `standards`. For reviews this is the axis; no separate `axis` field is required. |\n| `ticket` | Assignment string, including leading zeros (for example `\"01\"`). |\n| `attempt` | Assignment integer; stale attempts, booleans, floats, and strings are invalid. |\n| `base_revision` | Assignment's Git base commit string. |\n| `revision` | Assignment's current source commit string: starting source for implementation/repair, captured submission for reviews. |\n\nImplementation reports additionally require all of these fields:\n\n| Field | Required value/type |\n| --- | --- |\n| `result` | `\"submitted\"` or `\"human-decision-needed\"`; `\"Done\"` and other outcomes are invalid. |\n| `summary` | Nonempty string. |\n| `changed_files` | Array of nonempty strings describing claimed changes; may be empty. |\n| `checks` | Array of nonempty strings describing claimed checks; may be empty. |\n| `acceptance_evidence` | Array of nonempty strings; at least one entry for `submitted`. May be empty for `human-decision-needed`. |\n| `unresolved_questions` | Array of nonempty strings; at least one question for `human-decision-needed`. May be empty for `submitted`. |\n\nWhitespace-only strings are empty for validation purposes. Evidence is descriptive\ntext; the harness validates its shape and independently establishes Git changes\nand check results. It does not treat claims as verified facts. Workers edit their\nsource worktree and leave committing to the harness. Claimed revisions, file\nlists, checks, or execution state never override harness-owned observations.\n\nA complete `human-decision-needed` report stops the attempt with an incomplete\noutcome, even if the process exits nonzero. Describe the precise decision and\nrelevant approved requirement or interface in `summary`/`unresolved_questions`,\nand available evidence in `acceptance_evidence`. These existing fields remain the\nreport contract; no new report fields are required. Current work stays in its\nworktree without submission capture, further checks/reviews, integration, or repair.\n\nReview reports require a `findings` array, in addition to every identity field.\nAn empty array passes review only for the correct assignment and revision.\nEvery finding requires all of these fields, including optional suggestions:\n\n| Field | Required value/type |\n| --- | --- |\n| `id` | Nonempty stable string, unique within this review. |\n| `classification` | `\"blocking\"` or `\"optional\"`. |\n| `location` | Nonempty string identifying code or the closest location for missing behavior. |\n| `requirement` | Nonempty string identifying the requirement, project rule, or concrete design risk. |\n| `consequence` | Nonempty string describing the impact. |\n| `evidence` | Nonempty string supporting the finding. |\n| `resolution` | Nonempty string suggesting a proportionate resolution. |\n| `human_decision_needed` | JSON boolean (`true` or `false`), never an integer or string. |\n\nA complete blocking finding fails the gate while remaining a valid report.\nOptional findings are preserved and allow the gate to pass. Nonzero process exit\nfails even with a complete success report; zero exit cannot replace a valid report.\n\nA blocking finding with `human_decision_needed: true` from either review axis\nstops immediately, including on nonzero process exit. Use `requirement` to identify\nthe approved contract, `evidence`/`consequence` to explain the conflict, and\n`resolution` for the precise decision needed. The diagnostic includes the\nrequirement and resolution. A blocking decision finding takes precedence\nover ordinary repairable blockers in the same validated report. Optional findings\nretain their non-blocking behavior, including when their decision flag is true.\nThe entire report must pass identity, shape, and evidence validation before any\ndecision is recognized; invalid decision-like output follows ordinary bounded\nreport-failure handling. Report claims never become verified source/check facts.\n\nBoth kinds of decision leave the ticket Blocked and the run incomplete without\nconsuming the remaining repairs or launching subsequent verification processes.\nApproved spec, ticket, and standards stay unchanged; integration stays at its\nlast verified revision. Later-repair decisions retain all previous attempts,\nrejected revisions, prompts, and output. The harness hands off the conflict for\nhuman resolution rather than inferring requirements or changing an approved\ninterface. Starting a new run after a decision requires human-approved inputs;\nQA checkpoint continuation does not resume this blocked decision state.\n\nThe harness captures the submission, independently runs lightweight checks, and\nuses separate detached worktrees and processes for the two review axes. A failed\ngate rejects the attempt with preserved evidence and enters the remaining repair\nallowance. Lightweight-check failures, either blocking review axis, failed merges,\nand failed candidate suites can request repair. Missing/invalid reports and process\nfailures use this same allowance, with no secondary retries. Worker-internal\nediting/testing iterations consume only their single assignment.\n\nEvery repaired submission is captured as a new commit (even if a repair only\nrequires rerunning verification), with changed paths derived against the original\nbase. Applicable lightweight checks, both fresh reviews, and a new exact candidate\nsuite must pass. Rejected attempts' approvals never authorize the new source;\nsource validation starts again for the new attempt while retaining the shared\nbaseline check. Exhaustion leaves the ticket Blocked with an incomplete outcome,\nall rejected revisions/worktrees and full history intact. Baseline and preflight\nfailures start no attempts. Valid human-decision reports, approved-input\nor evidence integrity violations, worker-owned HEAD changes, and external\nintegration movement stop without authorizing more repairs. Repairs cannot change\nthe approved baseline or requirements. Timeouts share this same repair allowance.\nCheck and review worktrees must remain clean and on the submitted revision.\nAssume-unchanged and skip-worktree index flags are rejected so tracked edits\ncannot be concealed from source validation.\nThe harness rechecks earlier verified worktrees after each process and before\npromotion. Changed HEAD, index, or working files invalidate the affected gate:\n`passed` becomes false, `verification_status` is `invalidated`, and `diagnostic`\nidentifies the changed source. A structurally valid review report retains\n`report_status: \"valid\"`; it cannot approve changed source. This attempt stops;\nrenewed checks and reviews are required before integration. These observations\nare not an enforced process sandbox (ticket 36).\n\nThe harness creates a real merge candidate with the starting integration\nrevision as first parent, tests it, and atomically updates the integration ref\nusing the expected previous revision. In this sequential slice, submission\ndescends from the starting integration revision. Candidate parents must be that\nrevision and submission, and its tree must equal the reviewed submission tree;\nhook-created source resolutions require renewed review. The suite must leave\nthe exact candidate clean. Only that tested candidate becomes `done_revision`.\nAn external integration update causes compare-and-swap to fail; the harness\npreserves the external tip, candidate, and diagnostics. Failed merges and failed\ncandidate suites also leave integration unchanged by the harness. Caller HEAD,\nindex, and working files stay untouched. Git hooks are disabled for the final\natomic ref update so user code cannot run after final validation. Checkout,\nsubmission, and merge hooks execute within the integrity checks described below.\n\nApproved source files and snapshots are checked against recorded SHA-256\nidentities before processes and promotion. Each process and hook-capable Git\nworktree/submission/merge operation is bracketed by integrity\nchecks covering approved originals, snapshots, copies of approved paths in source\nworktrees, `run.json`, assignments, and earlier process artifacts. A submitted\nGit diff cannot change an approved input path, including through commit hooks.\nDetected edits stop the attempt. Gate or Git operation `integrity` entries record the path,\nexpected/observed SHA-256 (`null` for absent or unreadable file contents), and a rejected-content\nartifact when bytes exist. A directory replacing a harness-owned file is moved\nintact to its rejected artifact before restoration. Harness-owned snapshots and evidence are restored\nfrom the pre-process bytes after rejected bytes are saved; changed original\ninputs and source worktrees remain available for inspection. Restore original\nrequirements before starting a newly approved run.\n\nIntegrity checks observe process boundaries. They do not detect a transient edit\nrestored before the process returns, authenticate local artifacts, or prevent a\nprocess with unrestricted filesystem/Git access from racing observations. Run\nonly trusted commands until role access enforcement is supplied by ticket 36.\n\n## Optional review suggestions\n\nBoth review axes accept complete findings classified as `optional`. They do not\nfail a review, consume a repair allowance, or request a human decision, even when\ntheir `human_decision_needed` flag is true. Every optional finding still requires\nall documented finding fields and exact report identity. Whole-report validation\nand process success remain required: malformed optional evidence or a nonzero\nreview exit cannot approve a submission.\n\nRun and status print the same indented, human-readable JSON as `run.json`.\nRead each Spec/Standards gate's `report.findings` for suggestions, including its\nlocation, requirement, consequence, evidence and suggested resolution. For example,\na passing gate can contain `\"classification\": \"optional\"` while the ticket is\n`\"state\": \"Done\"`. The gate and report's `attempt` and `revision` identify the\nreviewed submission; `base_revision` identifies the fixed approved baseline.\n`ticket.done_revision` identifies the separately tested candidate merge.\n\nWhen blocking and optional findings coexist, the ordinary blocking failure\ntriggers bounded repair. The repair assignment's `repair_context.failure` names\nthe rejected stage, and `check_results` retains the complete review report.\nOptional findings in that history are context, not mandatory repair requirements.\nA blocking finding requiring a human decision still stops immediately under the\ndecision rules above. A passing repaired ticket may retain an unimplemented\noptional suggestion.\n\nEarlier gate reports and artifacts remain attached to their original reviewed\nrevisions, including after a successful repair or exhausted allowance. Each new\nsubmission requires renewed checks and fresh reviews. A suggestion may disappear,\nappear, or reuse an ID in a new report; its ID is unique only within that report.\nRead `attempts` and their gate ranges to distinguish earlier suggestions from the\nlatest review. No earlier finding or approval is transferred to a later revision,\nand retained history does not assert that a disappeared suggestion was resolved.\nStatus only reads these durable observations and launches no agents.\n\n## Stage timeouts and cleanup\n\nTimeouts are wall-clock process allowances, including assignment input delivery\nand output collection. Configure independent allowances with `timeouts`:\n\n| Key | Default seconds | Invocation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `implementation` | 1800 | Initial implementation assignment. |\n| `repair` | 1800 | Each later implementation assignment; role remains `implementation`. |\n| `spec` | 900 | Spec review on every attempt. |\n| `standards` | 900 | Standards review on every attempt. |\n| `checks` | 300 | Each configured lightweight check, separately. |\n| `baseline` | 1800 | Starting broader suite, attempt 0. |\n| `candidate` | 1800 | Exact candidate suite on every attempt. |\n\nFor example, `\"timeouts\": {\"checks\": 60, \"baseline\": 1200, \"candidate\": 2400}`\nuses defaults for all agent roles. Fractional seconds are allowed. The one-week\nupper bound avoids platform pipe-wait overflow; no timeout is disabled or infinite.\nExisting command arrays, assignment identities and report schemas are unchanged.\n\nEach invocation starts a new POSIX session/process group. At timeout the harness\nsends SIGKILL to that group, stopping the command and inherited descendants,\nincluding children that ignore SIGTERM. It collects available stdout/stderr as\nbytes into their artifacts. Output pipes also keep the deadline active if the\nleader exits while children retain output handles. Even a complete report and a\nzero leader exit cannot approve that timed-out stage or request a trusted human\ndecision: `report_status` is `not-evaluated`, with raw output retained for inspection.\nCompleted, non-timed-out reports retain whole-report validation and the existing\nhuman-decision precedence, including nonzero exits.\n\nFinal output draining and leader reaping each have a separate five-second cleanup\nbound. Failure to drain/reap or an unavailable executable is a shared execution\nenvironment failure: `outcome: \"environment\"`, no further repairs, and an\n`execution_error` on the gate. Ordinary stage timeouts enter the existing repair\nallowance or finish Blocked/incomplete when exhausted. A baseline timeout is an\nenvironment outcome with attempt 0 and no worker assignment. Cleanup runs before\nintegrity checks and repair copying, preserving stopped work and earlier evidence.\nThe group is also killed at normal command completion to stop leftover background\nchildren. Commands are trusted and must keep descendants in their inherited\nprocess group; this mechanism does not supply an OS sandbox or contain a process\nthat deliberately detaches into another session/group. Git operations/hooks are\nstill governed by the existing integrity checks; this ticket adds deadlines to\nconfigured worker/reviewer/check invocations, not arbitrary Git plumbing.\n\nEach gate records `timeout_category`, `timeout_seconds`, and `timed_out`, alongside\nits unchanged stage, actual attempt, revision, argv, output paths and exit code.\n`cleanup` identifies the `process_group`, `signal` (`SIGKILL`), and whether a signal\nwas `sent`. A timed-out leader may already have exit code 0; `timed_out: true`\nalways fails. The diagnostic names the stage and allowance. Attempts retain the\nfailure and gate range; run/status expose the resulting outcome and latest ticket\nstate. Repairs receive this complete gate/output evidence through `check_results`\nand the previous failure through `repair_context`; no timeout-specific retry loop\nor reuse of an earlier approval exists.\n\n`tests/test_timeouts.py` drives controlled finite scripted processes with a generous\nexternal CLI watchdog, asserting process/child termination through Linux `/proc`,\npartial output, report precedence, fresh repairs and exact candidate integration,\nexhaustion, preflight/baseline/environment failures, and evidence protection.\nSetting `TICKET_HARNESS_TEST_OBSERVATIONS` to a directory retains each case's actual\nrecord, output text, integration revision, and caller status before fixture cleanup.\n\n## Records and artifacts\n\n`run.json` records execution state (`Pending`, `Ready`, `Running`, `Verifying`, `Done`, or\n`Blocked`), attempt number, inputs, base/submission/candidate/Done identities,\nchanged files, gates and findings, final outcome, failures, and artifact paths.\n`max_repairs` records the normalized repair allowance; `timeouts` records all\nnormalized stage allowances in seconds. `attempts` is ordered and records\neach assignment's `attempt`, `starting_revision`, source worktree path, state\n(`Running`, `Verifying`, `Rejected`, or `Done`), observed submission/changed files\nand candidate when available, and a failure stage/message on rejection.\n`gate_start` (inclusive) and `gate_end` (exclusive, set when the attempt ends)\nindex that attempt's gates in the complete top-level `gates` array. Gate and\noperation `attempt` is zero for baseline work and positive for ticket attempts.\n`selected_tickets` lists selected canonical IDs in numeric order; `tickets` maps\neach to its observed state, attempt and available submitted/candidate/Done identities.\nContext-only tickets appear in `requirements`, never in this execution mapping.\n`integration_revision` records the last tip verified/promoted by this run; it is\nhistorical evidence if an external process later moves the branch. `base_revision`\nremains the run's starting base. The top-level `ticket` mirrors the active or last\nattempted ticket for compatibility (the configured primary before assignments).\nEarlier identities remain in `attempts`. Every attempt, gate and Git operation\ncarries `ticket` as its canonical ID; shared baseline gates/operations use null.\nGate ranges still index the complete run-wide `gates` array, and each ticket's\nattempt numbering and repair allowance start afresh. A successful repair retains rejected\nfailure evidence there while the run's final outcome is completed.\nWhen a valid report requests a human decision, the latest `ticket` and affected\n`attempts` entry also expose `human_decision`: the `role`, actual `attempt` and\nassigned `revision`, complete validated `report`, approved input paths/snapshots\nand SHA-256 identities in `approved_inputs`, and `assignment`, `stdout`, `stderr`\nartifact paths. The implementation source path remains on the attempt; the gate's\n`cwd` identifies the reporting worktree. The report retains the precise questions\nor complete review findings, including the contract and available evidence.\nThis handoff is descriptive evidence, not a passing gate or approval. A nonzero\nexit remains recorded and appears alongside the decision diagnostic. Attempt\ncounters and gate ranges include only assignments and processes actually run;\nthe stopped attempt is `Rejected` while the ticket is `Blocked`. Run's final JSON\nand read-only status display the same handoff and preserved history.\n`operations` records Git worktree preparation, submission capture, and candidate\nmerge stages, with `passed` and integrity diagnostics when applicable. These are\nseparate from check/agent gates; rejected observed revisions remain on the ticket\nor in the retained Git worktree if an operation failed before returning a revision.\nRecords are replaced atomically so status does not read a partially written\nsuccess. This does not provide crash recovery or resume.\n\n`artifacts/` holds each full assignment, standard output, and standard error;\ninitial names remain `implementation`, `spec`, `standards`, `check-NAME`, and\n`candidate`; later attempts prefix artifact/worktree/operation names with\n`attempt-2-`, `attempt-3-`, etc. This cannot collide with configured check names.\nFor multi-ticket selections, all ticket-specific artifact/worktree/operation\nnames additionally begin `ticket-ID-` (for example `ticket-03-attempt-2-spec`);\nthe shared baseline stays `baseline`. Single-ticket selections retain the existing\nnames. Earlier ticket evidence remains protected during later tickets and repairs.\nStage names and `TICKET_HARNESS_STAGE` retain their original role/check\nvalues. Prior artifacts are protected against overwrite by later processes and\nGit hooks. No attempt reuses a previous worktree or assignment process.\ngates record command arguments, working directory, revision, and exit code.\nAgent gates also record the parsed `report` when JSON decoding and the nesting\nlimit check succeed,\n`report_status` (`valid` or `invalid`), and a `diagnostic` on rejection. Report\nvalidity is separate from gate success: a valid report with a nonzero process\nexit, blocking finding, or human-decision outcome cannot pass. Invalid reports\nset `passed: false`; undecodable or excessively nested output is available verbatim through `stdout`.\n`failure` identifies the stage and reason. Run and status expose the same durable\nrecord, including rejected implementation evidence and both review axes.\nWorktrees and rejected Git commits remain available for inspection. To remove a\nrun after inspection, remove its worktrees with `git worktree remove` (use\n`--force` only for disposable rejected changes), then delete its output folder.\n",
-    ".agents/skills/custom/references/planning.md": "# Shared planning reference\n\nThis is the authoritative planning policy for custom planning workflows and\ntheir generated bundles. Upstream skills supply interview, domain, synthesis,\nand slicing procedures; this reference augments their outputs and governs when\npublication is allowed. It defines planning documents, not a runtime schema.\n\n## Context and draft boundary\n\nRead the target repository's `AGENTS.md`, domain glossary and relevant ADRs,\ntracker and triage conventions, testing policy, and existing feature inputs.\nUse project requirements and accepted architecture decisions over general design\nheuristics. Reuse existing custom guidance before proposing another policy.\nResolve environment facts by inspection; decisions belong to the human.\n\nFor this repository, read [domain guidance](../../../../docs/agents/domain.md),\n[tracker conventions](../../../../docs/agents/issue-tracker.md),\n[triage vocabulary](../../../../docs/agents/triage-labels.md), and\n[testing policy](../../../../docs/agents/testing.md) before the corresponding work.\nFor another target, use that project's equivalents; ask only for missing\ndecisions that cannot be inferred from its guidance or the conversation.\n\nKeep proposals in the conversation or an explicitly marked draft location,\nseparate from published inputs. A suitable local staging location is\n`.scratch/<feature>/drafts/<version>/`; mark each document **Draft \u2014 unapproved**.\nBefore approval, keep proposed individual ticket bodies inside `ticket-proposal.md`\nrather than publishing files under `issues/`. Existing approved spec, tickets,\nmilestones and index stay unchanged. Proposed glossary/ADR changes affecting an\napproved contract belong with the draft until accepted; ordinary resolved domain\nterms can be captured using the project's domain-modeling procedure.\n\nFor an existing plan, identify the approved version and its immutable inputs.\nDraft the proposed version separately, with an explicit change summary and\nunchanged requirements carried forward. Preserve completed-ticket identities,\nhistorical evidence, and prior versions. Active-run inputs stay immutable;\napproval of a revision permits a separate new version, not an in-place edit of\nthe active version. External publication requires its own authorization.\n\n## Grilling decisions and shared understanding\n\nUse grilling's frontier rounds: ask only decisions whose prerequisites are\nsettled, give recommendations, and wait for answers. Use domain modeling to\nresolve overloaded terms and test domain relationships with concrete scenarios.\nConsult [deep-module guidance](../../matt-pocock/codebase-design/SKILL.md) when\ninterfaces or ownership need design work. Record these decisions:\n\n- User problem, intended outcomes, actors, scope, and explicit exclusions.\n- Important interfaces, ownership of invariants, external dependencies, and\n  observable failure behavior. Leave internal implementation choices to workers.\n- Agreed behavioral testing seams, existing prior art, required check commands\n  or named gates, and evidence needed to establish success. Prefer existing\n  public seams; explicitly agree any new seam with the human using\n  [test strategy](../test-strategy/SKILL.md).\n- Required work versus independent experiments or optional follow-ups; genuine\n  prerequisite edges; ticket size and any prefactoring necessary for green slices.\n- Meaningful earlier capabilities for human QA, plus final acceptance; or the\n  user's every-N-ticket cadence. Set explicit membership, concrete human checks,\n  observable pass conditions, and how earlier gates constrain later work.\n- Worker/repair and separate Spec/Standards settings, ticket overrides, and\n  applicable role guidance. Resolve ambiguous settings before approval.\n\nSummarize settled decisions, proposed scope, testing seams, defaults and QA\nstrategy. The frontier must be empty for the proposed scope, and the user must\nconfirm shared understanding before synthesis. Confirmation authorizes drafting;\nit is distinct from approval to publish the eventual bundle.\n\n## Synthesis and joint revision\n\nUse to-spec's problem/solution/stories/decisions/testing structure and to-tickets'\nvertical slices and blocking edges. Each ticket delivers an independently\nverifiable outcome small enough for one fresh context. Mechanical wide refactors\nuse upstream expand\u2013migrate\u2013contract guidance when vertical slicing cannot land\ngreen. Specify contracts rather than prescribing internal classes or file layouts.\n\nPresent the spec and ticket/milestone proposal as one draft version. Include each\nproposed ticket's complete body, not only its title; the user must be able to\napprove the acceptance criteria, dependencies and exceptions before publication.\nAsk whether granularity and blocking edges are correct, which slices need merging\nor splitting, and whether the QA actions demonstrate the intended capabilities.\n\nApply feedback to the spec and proposal together. A changed outcome must update\nthe spec, affected ticket criteria/dependencies, milestone membership/QA, and any\ntesting or role settings it changes. Record the changes so the next approval\nidentifies the revised bundle. Pure clarification can be edited directly;\nconsequential new scope, interface, invariant, testing seam, dependency, or QA\ndecisions reopen only the affected grilling frontier and its dependent questions.\nConfirm the resulting shared understanding before synthesizing the new version.\n\n## Milestone membership\n\nAlways include a final milestone covering completion of every required ticket\nand overall feature acceptance. Its required membership can be only the final\ngroup; explicitly state cumulative coverage of earlier accepted groups. A single\nmilestone is reasonable for a small feature, but still propose earlier meaningful\ncapability checkpoints and explain when no useful earlier boundary exists.\n\nBy default group tickets into demonstrable capabilities. For an explicit every-N\nrequest, fix a topological ticket order using genuine dependencies and agreed\npriority, then partition it into groups of N with a smaller final group when\nneeded. For example seven tickets at N=3 produce groups 01\u201303, 04\u201306, and 07.\nRecord the actual IDs, not a dynamic rule such as \"whichever three finish next\".\nIf a requested order conflicts with dependencies, propose a valid order or ask\nthe human to resolve the conflict; do not quietly change N or invent edges.\n\nAssign every required ticket to exactly one required milestone. Dependencies may\npoint within its group or to an earlier group, never a later group. Milestone IDs\nremain stable across accepted versions; record any approved membership change.\nAn independent experiment can have a separate selection and final acceptance;\ndeclare its priority without making it a release prerequisite accidentally.\n\nFor each milestone give QA setup, actions the human can perform, expected\nobservable results, and acceptance evidence. Distinguish ticket Done, milestone\naccepted/promoted, and feature complete. Later groups wait for human acceptance\nof the previous group; all tickets Done still requires final QA. Plan approval\ndoes not accept a candidate. Actual request identities, candidates, decisions,\nexecution, promotion and continuation are owned by later runtime/QA workflows.\n\n## Ticket numbering\n\nBy default, number a new local ticket bundle consecutively in its preferred\nexecution order, with blockers before dependents and required milestones in\ntheir planned order. Start at 01 for a fresh feature; continue after any existing\nIDs when adding tickets. Within a milestone, use agreed priority to order\nindependent tickets. Numbering expresses a readable preferred sequence; retain\ngenuine dependency edges so independent work can still run in parallel.\n\nPlace an independent experiment at its intended scheduling point in that\nsequence while keeping its separate membership and acceptance. For example,\nif an experiment follows the backend slice, give it the next number before\nlater release work; numbering alone must not make it a release prerequisite.\nHuman QA gates still determine when later milestones become eligible.\n\nSettle the draft order before assigning final IDs. When feedback reorders,\nsplits or adds draft tickets, refresh their numbers, filenames, dependency\nlinks, milestone membership, proposal and index together before approval.\nPresent the numbered proposal in ascending order so the human can inspect\nthe intended sequence without a separate reordering request.\n\nFor revisions, preserve already published IDs, completed-ticket evidence and\ncaptured run inputs. If preserving published IDs prevents sequential order,\nshow the exception explicitly. Renumber published open tickets only when the\nhuman requests it; retain an old-to-new mapping and update live references\nwithout rewriting historical identities or active-run inputs.\n\n## Role and testing contracts\n\nRecord these defaults in the bundle unless the human chooses explicit alternatives:\n\n| Role | Reasoning | Configuration |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Implementation and repair | Medium | One worker setting; a ticket worker override applies to both roles. |\n| Spec reviewer | High | Independently configured from worker and Standards. |\n| Standards reviewer | High | Independently configured from worker and Spec. |\n\nRecord the agreed model for each role; do not silently guess model availability.\nUse fresh assignments, and record any agreed active-context target. For each\nticket, state its worker override and reason or explicitly inherit the default.\nA worker override never changes reviewer settings. Record local source references\nfor required guidance, project defaults by role, and ticket additions by role.\nAdditions supplement defaults and preserve required role/output/access contracts.\nUnselected installed skills are not automatically run inputs. Runtime skill\nresolution/capture and model-profile execution remain separate work.\n\nPreserve agreed interfaces, invariants and testing seams in the spec, with each\nticket linking its relevant contracts and checks. State behavioral coverage and\nrequired gates without duplicating implementation details. QA actions complement\nautomated gates; neither replaces the other. A new testing seam or changed check\nrequirement is a planning decision, not an internal worker convenience.\n\n## Complete bundle shape\n\nFor the local Markdown tracker, publish the following under `.scratch/<feature>/`\n(or the target's configured versioned destination). Use actual values throughout;\nthe fields below are a checklist, not executable metadata. Link relative paths\nand section anchors so a fresh worker can find the approved contracts.\n\n### `spec.md`\n\n- Title, plan version, approval record/reference, and relationship to any prior version.\n- **Problem Statement**, **Solution**, and numbered **User Stories** covering the\n  agreed behavior, failures and actors.\n- **Implementation Decisions**: agreed interfaces, invariants, ownership and\n  constraints; applicable domain/design references; deferred internal choices.\n- **Testing Decisions**: agreed seams, prior art, behavioral coverage, required\n  commands or named gate references, and expected evidence.\n- **Role Configuration**: model/reasoning defaults for worker/repair, Spec and\n  Standards independently; context targets and required/default guidance sources.\n- **Milestone Contract**: link to explicit membership, QA and final coverage.\n- **Out of Scope** and **Further Notes**, including unresolved matters if still\n  a draft. Required decisions must be resolved before approval/publication.\n\n### `ticket-proposal.md`\n\n- Draft/approved state and version matching the spec; summary of changes from\n  the prior proposal and any approved version.\n- Each ticket's full proposed body using the individual-ticket shape below.\n- Dependency graph or edge list, preferred topological order, milestone table\n  with actual ticket IDs, and the role/testing exceptions being proposed.\n- Questions/feedback and their resolution; approval scope and decision reference\n  once accepted. After publication link the individual ticket files.\n\n### `issues/<NN>-<slug>.md` (one per approved ticket)\n\n```markdown\n# <NN>: <Outcome title>\n\n**What to build:** <complete bounded end-to-end behavior>\n**Blocked by:** <numbers, titles and links, or None (can start immediately)>\n**Status:** <target triage label; ready-for-agent only when sufficiently specified>\n**Plan version:** <approved version; link to spec>\n**Milestone:** <stable ID; link to membership and QA>\n**Worker reasoning:** <inherit Medium, or explicit override and rationale; applies to repair>\n**Role guidance:** <inherit project defaults; additions by role with local source references>\n**Context:** <scoped spec contracts, domain/design references, agreed seam and check references>\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] <observable requirement and evidence that verifies it>\n- [ ] <failure/constraint requirement where applicable>\n\n## Verification\n\n<agreed behavioral seam, required checks and evidence; link shared requirements>\n```\n\nApply [ticket numbering](#ticket-numbering). Readiness is separate from satisfied\ndependencies or execution state. New acceptance markers remain unchecked. Include\na model/context override here if agreed; absent overrides inherit the spec defaults.\n\n### `milestones.md`\n\n- Version and approval reference, stable ordered IDs, named capabilities, fixed\n  required ticket membership and gate into each next group.\n- For each group: QA setup/actions, expected results and observable acceptance\n  conditions, required evidence, and initial state **not yet requested/accepted**.\n- Final checkpoint identified explicitly, with cumulative coverage of all\n  required work and overall feature acceptance.\n- Separate optional/experiment membership and priority where applicable.\n\n### `README.md` (feature index)\n\n- Version, approval scope/reference, links to spec, proposal and milestones.\n- Every ticket's ID/title/link, declared dependencies, readiness and milestone;\n  preferred execution order and current eligible group without claiming execution.\n- Final QA requirement and distinction between plan approval and QA acceptance.\n- Link prior versions/change summary for revisions; preserve existing completion\n  evidence. A published plan alone does not claim any ticket or milestone complete.\n\n## Approval, validation and publication\n\nBefore requesting publication approval, show the complete current spec and\nproposal, including the ticket bodies, milestone definitions, defaults/overrides\nand testing contracts. Identify version and intended destination. Explain that\nthe decision covers the coherent bundle; approval of scope, one ticket, or shared\nunderstanding alone does not approve publication of unseen material.\n\nUse an explicit human approval that identifies this current bundle. Existing\nauthorization suffices when it clearly covers the material being published;\ndo not ask again merely because a stage changed. If approval is declined,\nwithheld, ambiguous, or only partial, retain the draft and report the unresolved\nscope. No individual tickets, canonical spec, milestones or index are published\nuntil coherent-bundle approval is established. Silence is not approval.\n\nValidate the approved draft before publication:\n\n- Every agreed outcome maps to spec behavior and verifiable ticket criteria;\n  spec/proposal/ticket defaults, overrides, testing contracts and QA agree.\n- IDs and dependencies exist and are unique; graph is acyclic, and declared\n  external prerequisites have explicit completion requirements.\n- New ticket numbers follow the preferred execution sequence and milestone\n  order, with blockers before dependents and experiments at their agreed\n  scheduling points. Any preserved-ID exception is explicit; proposal order,\n  filenames, headings, dependency links, milestones and index agree.\n- Every required ticket has exactly one membership, dependencies respect group\n  ordering, cadence matches the request, and final cumulative coverage is complete.\n- Every milestone has actionable QA and acceptance conditions; no contradictory\n  settings, unresolved required decisions, or fabricated acceptance remain.\n- References resolve, ticket bodies match those approved, and the destination\n  preserves existing approved inputs and unrelated work.\n\nIf validation requires a substantive change, revise the draft and obtain approval\nfor that changed scope. Publish all files of the validated bundle, then check\ntheir cross-links, version, membership and criteria agree and record the human's\napproval reference without inventing a decision or timestamp. Report paths and\nany remaining execution prerequisites. This workflow authors approved inputs;\nit does not implement scheduling, branch promotion, approval watching, restart,\nQA execution, background launch, or runtime skill selection. Generated-bundle\nruntime and skill-metadata proof remains ticket 37 in the harness project.\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/12-deliver-focused-implementation-and-spec-review-contexts.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 12; use the [current ticket](../../issues/32-deliver-focused-implementation-and-spec-review-contexts.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/12-deliver-focused-implementation-and-spec-review-contexts.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 12: Deliver focused implementation and Spec-review skills\n\n**What to build:** Implementation, repair, and Spec-review assignments receive distinct repo-owned guidance, applicable project-default and ticket-added skills, and bounded requirements, code pointers, and evidence relevant to their roles.\n\n**Blocked by:** [11: Supply combined Python design guidance to Standards review](11-supply-combined-design-guidance-to-standards-review.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** First usable release.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Agent contracts and access](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#agent-contracts-and-access); [Custom skills and design policy](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#custom-skills-and-design-policy); [Skill selection and delivery](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#skill-selection-and-delivery); [Codex execution](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#codex-execution).\n\n**Scope:** The custom implementation and Spec-review roles and their context assembly, reusing issue 11's selection/capture/delivery contract, shared design policy, and Standards role. Adapt reusable guidance from existing custom skills while preserving the harness's ownership of checks, review orchestration, commits/integration, execution records, and completion.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Create an implementation skill that follows one ticket's approved contracts, uses agreed behavioral test seams, reports evidence, and surfaces decisions outside scope; reuse custom test strategy to assess existing coverage before TDD for missing behavior, preserving configured verification gates.\n- [ ] Create a Spec-review skill focused on missing, incorrect, and unrequested behavior against the approved spec and ticket.\n- [ ] Give every implementation, repair, and review assignment fresh context with required role instructions, captured project defaults, applicable ticket additions, and scoped references. Reuse the run's captured content for repairs even if source skills change.\n- [ ] Supply implementation-relevant design constraints without copying every upstream skill or unrelated ticket into its prompt.\n- [ ] Supply Spec review with the exact reviewed revision, approved acceptance requirements, relevant source/diff, and actual check results.\n- [ ] Keep development-wrapper bookkeeping, nested-review orchestration, and next-agent handoff authority outside runtime worker instructions; any adapted practices preserve harness role/access/output contracts.\n- [ ] Record the selected role skills, their selection origins, and the source/content identities actually supplied to each assignment; demonstrate role-appropriate defaults and ticket additions across a separate target repository, including a fresh repair assignment.\n\n## Verification\n\nUse skill-authoring checks and public scripted-agent runs that capture each role's inputs and produce schema-valid results; verify role separation, additive selection, stable captured guidance across repair, and preserved harness authority through observable assignment contents and outcomes. Reuse issue 11's common selection/capture coverage and add only distinct role or repair behavior.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/16-demonstrate-the-first-usable-release.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 16; use the [current ticket](../../issues/38-demonstrate-the-first-usable-release.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/16-demonstrate-the-first-usable-release.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 16: Demonstrate the first usable release from a clean checkout\n\n**What to build:** A developer can install the harness, configure a separate small repository, and reproduce the documented successful, repaired, and blocked outcomes.\n\n**Blocked by:** [07: Report optional review suggestions without rework](../../issues/07-report-optional-suggestions-without-repair.md); [10: Continue unrelated tickets after a blocked prerequisite](10-continue-independent-work-after-blocks.md); [14: Verify implementation and review access boundaries](14-verify-role-write-boundaries.md); [15: Produce executable Markdown requirements with a planning skill](15-produce-approved-harness-inputs-with-planning-skill.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** First usable release.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Testing decisions](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#testing-decisions); [Skill selection and delivery](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#skill-selection-and-delivery); [Out of scope](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#out-of-scope); [Public interface and approval](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#public-interface-and-approval).\n\n**Scope:** Release acceptance and the runnable walkthrough for the agreed sequential first version; preserve concurrency, recovery, and notifications as later work. The context-limit experiment is an independent early slice after issue 13, rather than a prerequisite for this release demonstration.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Document installation, project commands, role settings, limits, local requirements, run/status usage, and artifact inspection using the interfaces actually implemented.\n- [ ] Document required role guidance, project-default skills, additive ticket skills, supported local sources, required-reference validation, and run-captured content using the implemented metadata and source-resolution convention; show where to inspect actual delivered content and its identities.\n- [ ] Provide a runnable example showing successful integration, bounded repair, a blocked ticket, a waiting dependent, optional suggestions, and unrelated successful work.\n- [ ] Run the complete deterministic suite from a clean checkout without requiring model credentials or network access.\n- [ ] Run the separate small live check in the configured Codex environment and record actual integration and skill/access evidence, or report the explicit blocker without claiming complete live validation.\n- [ ] Show approved inputs, including captured selected skills/references, unchanged and integration references bound only to passing candidates; the walkthrough includes a project default and a ticket addition without relying on automatic discovery or installation.\n- [ ] Describe sequential execution and non-resumable interrupted runs accurately, with a clean distinction between the first release and deferred slices.\n\n## Verification\n\nFollow the documented walkthrough from a clean environment against disposable target repositories, collect real command outcomes, and reconcile them with status/report/Git observations.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/17-compare-configurable-agent-context-limits.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 17; use the [current ticket](../../issues/35-compare-configurable-agent-context-limits.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/17-compare-configurable-agent-context-limits.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 17: Compare configurable 100K\u2013250K agent context limits\n\n**What to build:** An early live-backend experiment configures 100K, 150K, 200K, and 250K per-agent active-context limits and compares verified outcomes on a fixed workload.\n\n**Blocked by:** [13: Complete one real Codex ticket with configured role profiles](13-run-a-real-codex-ticket-cycle.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** Early experiment immediately after 13; separate from first-release completion requirements.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Early context-budget experiment](../../README.md#early-context-budget-experiment); [baseline spec](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md).\n\n**Scope:** Extend the first verified real-Codex cycle with context controls and measurements. Prioritize this issue as soon as 13 is integrated; final release packaging, custom planning, and the comprehensive access-boundary probes are not prerequisites. Preserve this issue's stable identifier and avoid introducing automatic model selection.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Expose configurable per-agent context limits of 100000, 150000, 200000, and 250000 tokens with 250000 as the starting target.\n- [ ] Verify what the installed backend actually enforces or signals at each setting; distinguish enforced limits, compaction thresholds, and advisory targets instead of claiming an unsupported hard cap.\n- [ ] Measure observed peak active context separately from cumulative input/output usage and cached-input counts, recording role, attempt, limit, model, and reasoning settings.\n- [ ] Run the same bounded fixture workload and acceptance suite at each setting with consistent model/role settings and documented repetitions.\n- [ ] Include a short control and a task-relevant context-pressure case capable of approaching the configured thresholds. Distinguish policy-wiring checks from comparative runs that actually exercise a limit; do not infer an optimal budget from runs that remain below every threshold.\n- [ ] Report verified completion, failures, repairs, compactions or context-pressure handoffs, elapsed time, and cumulative usage alongside observed peak context.\n- [ ] Prevent context pressure from skipping checks or producing false completion; record unsupported enforcement or missing measurements as explicit limitations.\n- [ ] Publish a reproducible comparison and evidence-based recommendation without silently changing the established defaults.\n\n## Verification\n\nExercise the configured limits using the existing public CLI/live backend and fixed disposable-repository workload, retaining raw usage evidence and independent acceptance results.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/10-continue-independent-work-after-blocks.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 10; use the [current ticket](../../issues/29-continue-independent-work-after-blocks.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/10-continue-independent-work-after-blocks.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 10: Continue unrelated tickets after a blocked prerequisite\n\n**What to build:** A blocked ticket leaves dependents waiting while unrelated ready tickets finish, and the run ends with an accurate incomplete-work report.\n\n**Blocked by:** [06: Stop for decisions outside approved contracts](../../issues/06-surface-human-decisions-without-retrying.md); [09: Execute verified dependencies in a stable order](../../issues/09-execute-dependencies-sequentially.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** First usable release.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Ticket lifecycle](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#ticket-lifecycle); [Bounded failures and timeouts](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#bounded-failures-and-timeouts); [Public interface and approval](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#public-interface-and-approval).\n\n**Scope:** Failure isolation and termination of the existing sequential dependency scheduler.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Keep a ticket requiring a human decision or exhausting repairs Blocked; keep its dependent tickets Pending rather than runnable.\n- [ ] Continue unrelated ready tickets from the latest verified integration revision without retrying the blocked ticket implicitly.\n- [ ] Stop when no executable work remains and distinguish Done, Blocked, and Pending tickets in status and the final report.\n- [ ] Return a nonzero incomplete outcome when selected work remains unresolved, even if unrelated tickets succeeded.\n- [ ] A shared environment failure stops the run instead of repeatedly failing every remaining ticket.\n- [ ] Demonstrate one blocked root, one waiting dependent, and one unrelated successful ticket in the same fixture run.\n\n## Verification\n\nExercise exhausted-repair and human-decision fixtures through a real multi-ticket run; inspect branch history, launched assignments, waiting states, and the final outcome.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/11-supply-combined-design-guidance-to-standards-review.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 11; use the [current ticket](../../issues/30-supply-combined-design-guidance-to-standards-review.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/11-supply-combined-design-guidance-to-standards-review.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 11: Supply combined Python design guidance to Standards review\n\n**What to build:** Standards review receives a repo-owned skill and coherent Python design policy that reuse applicable custom, Matt Pocock, and Microsoft guidance. A shared skill-selection contract captures project defaults and ticket additions for delivery across repositories.\n\n**Blocked by:** [03: Reject invalid agent reports and review evidence](../../issues/03-validate-agent-reports-and-review-evidence.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** First usable release.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Custom skills and design policy](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#custom-skills-and-design-policy); [Skill selection and delivery](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#skill-selection-and-delivery); [Cohesive module responsibilities](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#cohesive-module-responsibilities); [Agent contracts and access](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#agent-contracts-and-access).\n\n**Scope:** Establish common selection, validation, capture, and delivery of local skill content; create the shared code-design guidance and Standards-review role through the existing assignment seam. Document canonical project-default and ticket-addition metadata and source resolution. Use skill-creation and writing-for-agents guidance when authoring skills. Issue 12 applies this foundation to implementation and Spec review; automatic discovery and installation remain outside scope.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Audit existing custom guidance and create one authoritative combined policy with focused references for deep modules, information hiding, responsibility/invariant ownership, composition, substitutability, justified dependency injection, and behavioral testing; record which existing guidance is reused and where role adaptation is needed.\n- [ ] Explicitly support Pythonic functions, objects, and combinations; avoid mandating patterns, class hierarchies, or speculative extension points.\n- [ ] Define a Standards-review skill that explains blocker evidence and consequences, keeps optional suggestions non-blocking, respects target-project standards and accepted architecture decisions, and incorporates the custom test-strategy review guidance without adding a reviewer or gate.\n- [ ] Support explicit local skill sources from the harness library, target repository, and already installed libraries. Project defaults apply by role; optional ticket additions supplement them while required role contracts remain present. Preserve compatibility for tickets without additions.\n- [ ] At run start, capture selected skill content and required references with their role, selection origin, source identity, and content identity; report unavailable required content before affected work starts. Later source edits leave the captured instructions unchanged.\n- [ ] Deliver captured policy, selected skills, and applicable target-project references explicitly to a Standards assignment and record the content actually delivered instead of assuming cross-repository skill discovery.\n- [ ] Retain the installed upstream libraries as references rather than editing or duplicating competing authorities.\n- [ ] Demonstrate through captured scripted-agent inputs and review outputs in a different target repository that defaults and a ticket addition reach the appropriate role, required contracts remain present, missing skills/references are rejected, and edits to source skills/references after capture do not alter delivered content.\n\n## Verification\n\nValidate the skills using their supported authoring checks, inspect concrete good/bad design examples, and exercise the public assignment path with captured scripted inputs and correctly identified review results. Map the selection/capture scenarios to existing CLI/Git/report coverage and extend it for missing behavior; retain relevant prompts and artifacts from those executions.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/14-verify-role-write-boundaries.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 14; use the [current ticket](../../issues/36-verify-role-write-boundaries.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/14-verify-role-write-boundaries.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 14: Verify implementation and review access boundaries\n\n**What to build:** The live backend enforces writable implementation worktrees and read-only reviewer source while protecting approved inputs, run records, and integration ownership.\n\n**Blocked by:** [13: Complete one real Codex ticket with configured role profiles](13-run-a-real-codex-ticket-cycle.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** First usable release.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Agent contracts and access](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#agent-contracts-and-access); [Codex execution](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#codex-execution); [Requirements, configuration, and run records](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#requirements-configuration-and-run-records).\n\n**Scope:** Exercise and tighten role permissions using the installed backend in disposable repositories; preserve the established harness-owned commit/integration model.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Verify that an implementation worker can make intended source changes in its assigned worktree.\n- [ ] Verify reviewer source access is read-only and does not require source mutation to produce its report.\n- [ ] Attempt controlled out-of-scope writes to disposable sentinel locations representing sibling source, approved requirements, and harness state; verify they are refused or detected before any acceptance.\n- [ ] Keep shared Git integration references and authoritative run-state changes under harness ownership.\n- [ ] Reject an assignment if required access/instruction delivery cannot be established; do not silently widen permissions to make the fixture pass.\n- [ ] Record concrete backend/sandbox evidence and keep caller work and approved requirements unchanged.\n\n## Verification\n\nCombine deterministic role-configuration tests with explicit live sandbox probes against disposable sentinels. Inspect the actual files/references and role results rather than accepting the agent's own permission claims.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/issues-before-milestone-qa/13-run-a-real-codex-ticket-cycle.md": "# Historical ticket definition\n\nSuperseded by the approved QA-first plan. This is the previous unimplemented definition of ticket 13; use the [current ticket](../../issues/34-run-a-real-codex-ticket-cycle.md) for work. Source: `84982495be5d4943301e165f8419421391e75d18:.scratch/ticket-harness/issues/13-run-a-real-codex-ticket-cycle.md`. Relative links were adjusted for this archive location.\n\n# 13: Complete one real Codex ticket with configured role profiles\n\n**What to build:** A small ticket completes the existing verified cycle with actual fresh Codex implementation and review processes in a separate disposable target repository.\n\n**Blocked by:** [08: Stop stalled assignments and check processes](../../issues/08-stop-timed-out-process-trees.md); [12: Deliver focused implementation and Spec-review skills](12-deliver-focused-implementation-and-spec-review-contexts.md)\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n**Phase:** First usable release.\n\n**Agent context limit:** 250,000 tokens of active context. Use the [agent context policy](../../README.md#agent-context-policy).\n\n**Context:** [Codex execution](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#codex-execution); [Skill selection and delivery](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#skill-selection-and-delivery); [Agent contracts and access](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#agent-contracts-and-access); [Public interface and approval](../spec-before-milestone-qa.md#public-interface-and-approval).\n\n**Scope:** Replace the scripted process at the existing external seam with the installed Codex CLI; preserve all current gate and role contracts.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Verify the installed CLI invocation and launch fresh assignments without resuming or inheriting the parent development conversation.\n- [ ] Apply explicit working directory, role-specific model/reasoning settings, sandbox, timeout, and output contract without silently substituting a different configured model.\n- [ ] Capture machine-readable events and schema-validated final results as run artifacts.\n- [ ] Explicitly deliver captured custom implementation, Spec-review, Standards-review, and applicable design instructions across the repository boundary, including a project-default custom skill and a ticket-specific addition for its selected role; record source/content identities and actual delivered instructions.\n- [ ] Demonstrate a small actual Codex implementation followed by separate actual reviews and successful exact-candidate integration.\n- [ ] Keep routine tests scripted and offline; make the authenticated real-Codex check explicit and record actual evidence or a concrete environment blocker rather than counting a skipped check as success.\n\n## Verification\n\nRun existing deterministic checks and the separate tiny real-Codex fixture. Report the CLI version/settings, approved role selections and actual supplied content identities, check outcomes, reviewed/candidate revisions, and observed final integration. Use the small live cycle to demonstrate availability and an observable response to task-relevant selected guidance; retain captured instructions and reports. Keep exhaustive missing-reference, source-edit, and repair variations in deterministic coverage from issues 11\u201312 rather than multiplying live runs.\n\n## Handoff\n\nReturn the implemented behavior, changed commits/files, checks and results, and unresolved questions in a compact report. Follow the [shared worker handoff](../../README.md#worker-handoff).\n",
-    ".scratch/ticket-harness/archive/ticket-numbering-history.md": "# Ticket numbering history\n\nHistorical IDs and membership retain their earlier meaning. Current-owner links follow the [ownership/review amendment map](oop-numbering-history.md). Use [current milestones](../milestones.md) for eligibility.\n\nThe human requested sequential execution numbers after approving and publishing the QA-first plan. This refactor changes the identities of its 19 unimplemented tickets, preserving their scope, dependencies, acceptance criteria and readiness. Completed tickets 01\u201309 and their evidence remain unchanged. No implementation or human QA acceptance is implied.\n\nThe previous QA-first IDs below refer to the published QA realignment before sequential renumbering. The older [pre-QA definitions](issues-before-milestone-qa/) are a different historical stage: their IDs, requirements and dependency text remain historical. Their \u201ccurrent ticket\u201d pointers lead to the present owner; original 11 and 15 were split across two current tickets each, as the [approved plan](../ticket-proposal.md#comparison-with-the-pre-qa-open-tickets) explains.\n\n| Previous QA-first ID | Current ID | Stable plan slice | Current ticket |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 15 | 10 | R01 | [Turn one grilling session into an approved plan](../issues/10-produce-approved-harness-inputs-with-planning-skill.md) |\n| 18 | 11 | R02 | [Stop development handoffs at human checkpoints](../issues/11-stop-development-handoffs-at-qa-milestones.md) |\n| 19 | 12 | R03 | [Execute only the first eligible milestone](../issues/12-execute-only-the-current-qa-milestone.md) |\n| 20 | 13 | R04 | [Present a tested feature-to-dev candidate](../issues/13-prepare-a-tested-feature-to-dev-candidate.md) |\n| 21 | 14 | R05 | [Consume approval, promote, and continue](../issues/14-promote-only-the-human-approved-milestone.md) |\n| 22 | 15 | R06 | [Continue after a deliberate QA stop](../issues/15-continue-from-a-planned-qa